WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.509
Hello, everybody. This is Peter Cooper, from
the NCBI service desk and welcome to another

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:12.940
NCBI webinar. One of the shorter ones, the
one that we call the NCBI minute so we will

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:19.250
talk about 10 minutes here. Some reminders,
and that is what this slide is for. Recording

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:24.050
of this webinar will be available on the YouTube
channel and it will take us about 10 days

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:30.420
to get that up there. The materials, which
basically our backup slides, kind of screenshots

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.340
that we will show you, are available on the
FTP site and that compressed URL will take

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:42.250
you to those materials. Today Melissa Landrum,
who is the lead developer for the ClinVar

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.649
resource is going to be talking about the
new ClinVar submission wizard and I am going

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:49.579
to turn it over to Melissa right now.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.420
Thank you, Peter, and hello everyone, thank
you for joining us today. ClinVar is the NCBI

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.710
database for archiving interpretations of
variants relative to diseases and other phenotypes.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:05.150
All interpretations in ClinVar are provided
by our submitters, including clinical testing

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:11.369
labs, research labs, resources like OMIM,
GeneReviews and locus specific databases as

00:01:11.369 --> 00:01:14.640
well as expert panels and practice guidelines.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:22.140
A link to information on how to submit to
ClinVar is available from the ClinVar homepage.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.280
Submitters who have several interpretations
to submit or submitters that submit repeatedly

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.380
like clinical testing labs use our submissions
spreadsheet templates and there is a section

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.650
on this page with information about those
templates. For researchers and other submitters

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.740
who submit a single variant at a time, it
may be easier to use the ClinVar submission

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.570
wizard to guide you through the submission
process. For example, a genetic counselor

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:52.140
may review a variant in a lab report and want
to submit her own interpretation to ClinVar

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:57.149
or a research lab might be publishing a paper
on a new causative variant for a disease.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:02.149
To get to the wizard from this page, you can
click on how to submit, and you will see a

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:09.110
link here to NCBI's Variation Submission Portal.
We will click on that. Also note that you

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:14.020
can get to the submission portal from the
NCBI homepage.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.069
For this demonstration today, I will switch
to an internal site, but note that when you

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:24.140
use the submission portal, you will not need
to make any changes to the URL. When you first

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.879
log into the Variation Submission Portal,
you will need to register your organization.

00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:34.599
You can use this help link at the top of the
page if you need more information to do that.

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:39.670
Once your organization is registered and approved,
on this page, you will see the name of your

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:44.950
organization, so -- this is the demo -- by
organization name is ClinVar submission test

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:51.370
lab. That will be followed by two buttons,
one for the ClinVar a single submission wizard,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.739
and the second is to upload a new file submission
like a spreadsheet.

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:03.610
So today, we are going to click on the button
for the submission wizard. The wizard walks

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:08.599
you through a series of tabs for different
kinds of information. The first tab is for

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:13.209
general information about your submission.
Here you will provide a name for the submission,

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:18.090
which is just for your own use in submission
portal. You can use the name that is provided

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.530
or you can give your own name, so today, I'm
going to submit a variant that causes CFC

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:32.360
syndrome so I am going to name the syndrome
CFC 1 variant 1. If your submission is part

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.280
of a named to study like the seizure project
or that Framingham study, you can provide

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.170
the name and a description of the study in
these boxes.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:47.940
If your submission is from a standalone research
project, you can leave these boxes blank.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.519
You can let us know if you need your submission
held until the corresponding paper is published

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:57.659
by selecting hold until published here. For
this example, I'm going to select public so

00:03:57.659 --> 00:04:03.750
that my submission is not held. If the citation
is already available, you can provide that

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:08.780
information in this section. So today, for
my demonstration, I am going to provide a

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:17.949
publication using a pub med ID. Note that
on each tab, your work is not saved until

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:23.020
you click the save and continue button, but
that means that you can start a submission,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.310
save your work for one or more tabs and come
back to the form later to complete the process

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:35.419
so I will click save and continue. That takes
us into the next tab, where you can indicate

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:40.370
which organization should get attribution
for this submission. If your submission is

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.590
from a single organization, like a single
research lab, you can select single organization

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.750
here and save immediately the organization
for which you have logged on will be used

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:57.160
automatically. If your submission is for multiple
groups, each of those groups needs to be registered

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.500
and approved in the submission portal first
and you will need to have been defined as

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.410
a submitter for each organization. Then, when
you select multiple organizations, all of

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:12.940
those groups are available in this table.
One should be specified as the primary organization

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:19.130
for the ClinVar submission and any number
of others can be specified as additional organizations.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:25.039
For today's example I am going to submit from
a single organization. Use the next tab to

00:05:25.039 --> 00:05:30.960
define the variant that you have interpreted.
The interpreted variant may be a single variant,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:37.680
like a single nucleotide substitution, two
single variant as a compound heterozygote,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.070
or multiple variants in cis as a haplotype.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:47.240
To describe the location of the variant and
the change that you observed, you can use

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:54.330
either HGVS nomenclature or chromosome coordinates
here called sequence location. When you select

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.990
the types of variant and method of description,
the page updates to provide the appropriate

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:03.979
field for you to fill in. For this example,
I am submitting a single variant in the b-raf

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:09.880
gene so I'm going to describe with an HGVS
expression so I will fill in the reference

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:19.340
sequence with accession and version. A description
of the sequence change, so that would be the

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:27.360
c. or g. of an HGVS expression. I am going
to fill in the gene. Note that you can start

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.030
typing the gene symbol and you will get a
list from which to choose. I'm going to provide

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:37.979
an alternate designation. Here, this is the
protein name, but this could be any other

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:42.810
name by which that variant is known including
other nucleotide descriptions with legacy

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:48.620
numbering systems. There are other optional
fields to fill in. You can provide a variation

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:54.849
identifier like a dbSNP or rs number or the
location of the variant so the intron or exon

00:06:54.849 --> 00:07:00.949
that it is in, your local identifier for the
variant if you have a database, or the functional

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:05.900
consequence. Note that most of these fields
on this tab are optional so you can just fill

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:10.590
in the information that you have.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:15.780
Variants in ClinVar are generally interpreted
with respect to a disease or condition so

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.770
this condition is indicated on the conditions
tab. If you have information to submit on

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:26.210
observed phenotypes or clinical features,
please wait to submit them on the observations

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:31.639
tab. The condition for which you are interpreting
of the variant can be described with a database

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:39.729
identifier like a OMIM number. Or you can
provide the name of the disease in this box.

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:44.560
In some cases, a variant is submitted to describe
its effect on a person's response to a drug,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:51.120
so for these cases, you can select drug response
here, and the page updates to let you enter

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.379
the drug name, and optionally, the condition
for which that drug is used. For this example,

00:07:57.379 --> 00:08:03.949
I'm going to submit on the disease, CFC 1
syndrome and I'm going to do so with an OMIM

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:04.949
number.

00:08:04.949 --> 00:08:15.629
Use this tab to describe your interpretation
of the clinical significance of the variant.

00:08:15.629 --> 00:08:20.879
You must provide a value for the clinical
significance of the options for that are available

00:08:20.879 --> 00:08:26.449
in this pulldown menu. I am going to submit
that this variant is likely pathogenic. And

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:31.750
then, optionally, you can also provide other
information, such as the mode of inheritance

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:37.779
for that variant, again options are provided
in the menu. You can provide the date that

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:43.110
the clinical significance of that variant
was last evaluated. And you can also provide

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:48.570
a comment that describes why you classified
the variant as you did. This tab also led

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:54.040
to provide information about the method that
you used to classify variants which in ClinVar

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:59.640
we call your assertion criteria. More information
about this is available on the ClinVar website

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.200
using this link. But in short, this is a document
that describes the categories that you use

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.730
to classify variants such as benign, uncertain
and pathogenic and the criteria that you required

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:16.050
to assign a variant to each category. If this
information is in your publication describing

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:22.360
the variant, the pub med ID can be provided
as the assertion criteria here. We do encourage

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.209
submission of assertion criteria if it is
available because it affects the review status

00:09:27.209 --> 00:09:32.420
or number of stars on the resulting ClinVar
record. But it is not required so you can

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:40.959
certainly submit your data to ClinVar without
assertion criteria, leaving this section blank.

00:09:40.959 --> 00:09:45.930
ClinVar submissions also include the evidence
that supports the interpretation in the form

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:52.490
of observations of the variant. Those observations
it may be described as aggregates, individuals,

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:59.170
or experimental results. At least one observation
is required per submission. If you want to

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:04.320
submit data for many individuals as a group,
you can use the aggregate section. You can

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:09.510
submit information about more than one group
as distinct aggregates. For example if you

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:14.740
had information from a case-control study,
you can complete a section for the cases and

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:21.410
another for the controls. In other words,
all of the information in this observation

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:31.630
applies to everyone in this group. If you
prefer, you can provide information about

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:37.019
each individual with the variant. So in this
case, all of the information is specific to

00:10:37.019 --> 00:10:43.380
that person, such as that person's age, their
ethnicity, or the phenotypes that were observed

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:49.810
in that individual. For research groups, who
have performed functional assays to evaluate

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:55.320
a variant, that experimental evidence can
also be described. That information might

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:01.610
include the cell line used in the experiment
or the tissue type that was analyzed, and

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:08.350
you can also provide a comment that also describes
your experiment. More structured details about

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:16.380
the experimental methods can also be provided.
For this example, I am going to submit one

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:22.010
aggregate observation. So this is information
about a group of affected individuals for

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:28.970
this disease. I am going to name my observation.
I'm going to indicate, yes, that they were

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:34.640
affected with the disease. I am going to indicate
that the allele origin was germline, this

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:39.950
variant was detected as a germ line variant,
and indicate that the data was collected with

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:45.070
clinical testing. I am also going to indicate
that this was seen in a mixed group of males

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:53.540
and females, and the age range for these individuals
is from 1 to 20 years. I am going to indicate

00:11:53.540 --> 00:12:00.800
that I saw this variant in six families and
a total of six single heterozygotes.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.940
When you have entered in your information,
make sure to click save and continue and when

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:10.209
you have entered all of your observations,
click save and continue. Which takes you to

00:12:10.209 --> 00:12:15.500
the final tab. This is an overview of the
information that you have provided. You can

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:23.209
take this opportunity to review the data and
go back and correct anything if necessary.

00:12:23.209 --> 00:12:27.870
Once you are finished, click the submit button
at the bottom of the page. This is a critical

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:33.240
step. It sends your data to ClinVar and sends
you an email confirming that you have submitted

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:38.269
your data. When we receive the submission,
one of the ClinVar curators will check it

00:12:38.269 --> 00:12:43.820
and contact you with any questions about the
data. Otherwise, your data will be promptly

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:49.740
processed and made available on the ClinVar
website. We encourage you to try the ClinVar

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:54.930
Submission Wizard and we welcome your feedback.
You can use the contact link at the top of

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.579
this page to send us any questions or suggestions
about the wizard.

00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:04.920
We have time for a few questions. If anyone
wants to send those in, we would be happy

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.290
to answer them.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:13.430
The first question that we have is, how do
I know if I should use the wizard or use a

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.360
spreadsheet to submit to ClinVar?

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.570
Our recommendation is that if you are submitting
a very small number of variants at a time,

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:27.670
let's say less than 5, it will probably be
useful to use the wizard. If you are submitting

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:33.630
more variants, let's say more than 5 or 10
variants, you will get faster processing of

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:38.430
your submission if you use the spreadsheet
because that makes it more efficient for the

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:43.899
curator to review many variants at a time
and get back to you with any issues so that

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:46.260
it can be processed more quickly.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:52.269
The second question we have here is, if I
find that I have made a mistake in my submission,

00:13:52.269 --> 00:13:55.269
can I get in and edit it?

00:13:55.269 --> 00:13:59.889
If you have already sent your submission to
ClinVar, so here in the table, you can track

00:13:59.889 --> 00:14:03.680
the progress of your submission, so this is
the one that I just entered and you can see

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:08.640
that the status is submitted. It does not
have an edit button here so once you have

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:14.060
already sent it to ClinVar, it is not available
to edit. If one of our curators finds something

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:18.279
that needs to be fixed, we will change the
status so that there is an edit button here

00:14:18.279 --> 00:14:24.470
for you to fix your data. If you have not
yet submitted your wizard submission, you

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:28.630
will see an edit button here and you can go
in and make any edits until you are ready

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:29.630
to submit.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:35.750
We have another question. Should people only
use the wizard for submissions where they

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:40.990
have made their own assertion on the variant
or would clinicians use this to report assertions

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.760
directly from lab reports?

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:51.290
You can certainly use it to submit your own
assertion of the variant. Whether or not clinicians

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:57.750
can use it to report directly from lab reports.
You can certainly, Actually, for that, I would

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:02.480
actually suggest at this point that if you
would like to do that, you can use the spreadsheet

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:08.459
and the reason is that the labs themselves
may have already submitted that interpretation

00:15:08.459 --> 00:15:13.220
to ClinVar and, if you are going to submit
somebody elses assertion, we need to know

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:17.870
who the testing laboratory was. So that is
a feature that we will add to the wizard in

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:23.250
a future release, but for now, that information
can be submitted on the spreadsheet. So if

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:27.470
you would like to submit that kind of information
and you need help with the spreadsheet, please

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:30.920
do feel free to contact us.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:35.399
Okay. This is Peter Cooper. I am back again.
Thank you, Melissa. That was great and that

00:15:35.399 --> 00:15:41.480
is all we have time for today, so we will
have the slides and materials, sort of the

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:45.480
screenshots, available on the webinars and
courses page and we will have the recording

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.459
up on the YouTube channel probably within
10 days. Thank you, everybody, for coming

00:15:50.459 --> 00:15:52.110
and I am going to go ahead and end the webinar.

